    <p class="first dropcap"><span class="kick">MOSCOW &mdash;</span> Russia has decided to let its citizens buy bitcoin &mdash; carefully. President Vladimir Putin signed a law on August 4 legalizing regulated crypto trading under the Bank of Russia, and on August 11 the central bank cleared its first three assets for licensed exchanges: bitcoin, ether and Tether. XRP did not make the cut.</p>
<p>The gate is narrow. Non-qualified investors &mdash; ordinary Russians &mdash; may buy no more than 300,000 rubles, about $3,700, of these assets a year through any one intermediary, and only after passing a risk test. Qualified investors face no cap. Core provisions take effect September 1.</p>
<p>For a country under heavy sanctions, an onshore, surveilled bitcoin market is less about retail speculation than about control: liquidity the state can see, tax, and potentially tap.</p>
<p><em>Why it matters: the question is no longer whether governments allow bitcoin, but on what terms &mdash; and Russia&rsquo;s terms are a cage with the door left ajar.</em></p>
<p><a href="https://thebitcoinbeacon.com/stories/2026-08-17/russia-legalizes-bitcoin-retail-cap">Read the full dispatch &rarr;</a></p>

    <span class="sub">Network &amp; Mining</span>
    <p><span class="kick">ROCKDALE, Texas.</span> Riot Platforms, one of the largest listed bitcoin miners, signed a 20-year lease handing 191 megawatts at its Rockdale campus to the AI lab Anthropic &mdash; $9.1 billion in base rent, up to $16.1 billion if extensions are exercised. Riot will build and operate the halls; Anthropic brings the chips. The bitcoin miner is becoming an energy landlord, and it is not alone: across the sector, firms are selling coins and leasing megawatts as AI outbids hashing for cheap power. <a href="https://thebitcoinbeacon.com/stories/2026-08-17/bitcoin-miners-lease-power-to-ai-riot-anthropic">Full story &rarr;</a></p>

    <span class="sub">Markets &amp; Institutions</span>
    <p><span class="kick">NEW YORK.</span> Goldman Sachs agreed to acquire NEOS Investments for up to $2.25 billion, and the prize is BTCI &mdash; a roughly $1 billion fund that never buys bitcoin outright. It holds bitcoin exchange-traded products and sells covered calls against them, paying shareholders monthly income at an annualized rate in the mid-20s. The deal hands Goldman about $30 billion across 19 options-income ETFs and vaults it past BlackRock in the bitcoin-yield business. <a href="https://thebitcoinbeacon.com/stories/2026-08-17/goldman-neos-bitcoin-income-etf">Full story &rarr;</a></p>

    <span class="sub">Money &amp; Macro</span>
    <p><span class="kick">CHICAGO.</span> The trade that made bitcoin respectable on Wall Street &mdash; buy the spot ETF, short the CME future, pocket the premium &mdash; has stopped paying. As the futures basis compressed below the yield on two-year Treasuries, institutions pulled out: CME open interest fell to its lowest since February 2024, and Binance overtook it for the first time since 2023. The regulated venue is thinning while offshore fills the gap. <a href="https://thebitcoinbeacon.com/stories/2026-08-17/basis-trade-unwind-binance-overtakes-cme">Full story &rarr;</a></p>

    <span class="sub">Policy &amp; Nation-States</span>
    <p><span class="kick">SEOUL.</span> South Korea is herding bitcoin trading onshore. Its financial-intelligence unit pressed Google to pull dozens of overseas exchange apps &mdash; including OKX and Bybit &mdash; from new Android installs, cutting off roughly half the top global derivatives venues. In the same move, regulators softened a 20% ownership cap toward 34%, clearing mega-banks like KB and Shinhan to acquire domestic exchanges Upbit, Bithumb and Coinone. <a href="https://thebitcoinbeacon.com/stories/2026-08-17/south-korea-blocks-offshore-exchanges-banks">Full story &rarr;</a></p>

    <div class="take">
      <div class="tag">The Take &middot; Opinion</div>
      <h3>Wall Street Stopped Betting Bitcoin Goes Up</h3>
      <p>Line up this week&rsquo;s institutional moves and they point one way. Goldman paid up to $2.25 billion not to buy bitcoin but to own a fund that <em>sells options</em> on it. UBS multiplied its bitcoin call exposure while cutting puts &mdash; covered-call overwriting. Cboe filed to list 3x leveraged bitcoin funds. And the cash-and-carry trade that anchored the CME unwound because its premium fell below Treasury yields. None of that is a bet on a higher price.</p>
      <p>It is a bet on stillness. The smart money has stopped positioning for appreciation and started harvesting bitcoin&rsquo;s volatility for yield. The steelman is real: income products broaden the base and dampen forced selling, and flat is not bearish. But covered calls cap the upside they sell, and a market that monetizes chop is a market resigned to going nowhere. When the biggest players are done buying the thing and start renting out its variance, ask who is left to bid the top.</p>
      <p class="sig"><a href="https://thebitcoinbeacon.com/stories/2026-08-17/institutions-stopped-betting-bitcoin-up-take">Read the argument &rarr;</a> &bull; The Bitcoin Beacon</p>
    </div>

    <span class="sub">What We&rsquo;re Watching</span>
    <p>The CLARITY Act&rsquo;s Senate cloture vote on September 15, the closest thing to a US verdict on what bitcoin legally is. MSCI&rsquo;s November index review, which could force passive funds to dump bitcoin-treasury proxies like Strategy and Metaplanet. Whether other sanctioned or capital-controlled states copy Russia&rsquo;s legalize-but-cap template. And whether Riot&rsquo;s AI pivot spreads far enough among large miners to start bending the hashrate curve.</p>

